1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,970
You text a friend in London.

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:03,230
"Landed yet?"

3
00:00:03,830 --> 00:00:04,930
In under a second,

4
00:00:04,930 --> 00:00:05,520
it's there.

5
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:07,350
But to get there,

6
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:08,440
your message dove to

7
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:10,110
the bottom of the Atlantic Ocean.

8
00:00:11,080 --> 00:00:12,440
It rode a beam of light,

9
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:14,922
through a cable on the sea floor.

10
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:18,030
Now imagine every one of those cables snaps.

11
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:18,930
All of them.

12
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:19,970
At the same second.

13
00:00:20,780 --> 00:00:22,330
What happens in the first minute?

14
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:23,740
The first day?

15
00:00:23,740 --> 00:00:24,570
The first month?

16
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:28,440
What if every undersea internet cable was cut?

17
00:00:29,690 --> 00:00:30,300
First,

18
00:00:30,330 --> 00:00:31,700
what's actually down there.

19
00:00:32,430 --> 00:00:33,870
More than 600 cables,

20
00:00:33,870 --> 00:00:35,140
active and planned.

21
00:00:35,970 --> 00:00:37,460
Laid end to end,

22
00:00:37,510 --> 00:00:39,720
over 900,000 miles of them.

23
00:00:40,290 --> 00:00:41,510
That's enough to wrap around

24
00:00:41,510 --> 00:00:43,360
the Earth about 37 times.

25
00:00:44,070 --> 00:00:46,060
And most of the way across the ocean,

26
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:48,600
a cable is about as thick as a garden hose.

27
00:00:49,510 --> 00:00:50,410
Inside it:

28
00:00:50,410 --> 00:00:51,880
a few strands of glass,

29
00:00:52,010 --> 00:00:53,320
each thinner than a hair,

30
00:00:53,390 --> 00:00:54,780
carrying pulses of light.

31
00:00:55,930 --> 00:00:56,900
What about satellites?

32
00:00:57,670 --> 00:00:58,780
For the United States,

33
00:00:58,830 --> 00:01:01,330
satellites are just 0.37 percent

34
00:01:01,330 --> 00:01:02,920
of all international capacity.

35
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:05,230
The rest goes through cables.

36
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:08,270
And cables break all the time.

37
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:10,110
About 200 times a year.

38
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:12,210
Two thirds of those are accidents:

39
00:01:12,420 --> 00:01:13,680
fishing gear and ship anchors

40
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:16,010
dragging across the sea floor.

41
00:01:16,310 --> 00:01:16,780
Sharks?

42
00:01:17,070 --> 00:01:19,680
From 2007 to 2014,

43
00:01:19,730 --> 00:01:21,600
fish bites caused zero faults.

44
00:01:22,730 --> 00:01:24,580
One cut is no big deal.

45
00:01:24,710 --> 00:01:26,660
The internet just routes around it.

46
00:01:27,150 --> 00:01:28,270
But what if there's nothing

47
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:29,580
left to route around?

48
00:01:30,910 --> 00:01:31,680
Second one.

49
00:01:32,180 --> 00:01:34,230
Your message to London never arrives.

50
00:01:34,950 --> 00:01:36,270
Routers on the coast notice

51
00:01:36,270 --> 00:01:37,530
the dead links in a split

52
00:01:37,530 --> 00:01:39,280
second and look for another path.

53
00:01:40,190 --> 00:01:41,180
There isn't one.

54
00:01:41,390 --> 00:01:43,400
Every road off the continent is gone.

55
00:01:44,550 --> 00:01:45,380
Minute one.

56
00:01:45,530 --> 00:01:46,820
And here's the weird part:

57
00:01:47,050 --> 00:01:48,560
most of your phone still works.

58
00:01:49,290 --> 00:01:51,440
Texts to people nearby still go through.

59
00:01:51,750 --> 00:01:53,060
Your local news still loads.

60
00:01:53,710 --> 00:01:56,230
Big streaming services keep copies of popular

61
00:01:56,230 --> 00:01:58,400
shows on servers inside your country.

62
00:01:58,970 --> 00:02:00,500
So your show keeps playing.

63
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:03,610
But tap a website that only lives overseas,

64
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:04,550
and it spins.

65
00:02:04,840 --> 00:02:05,270
Forever.

66
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:08,440
Your video call to family abroad freezes,

67
00:02:08,610 --> 00:02:09,300
then drops.

68
00:02:10,450 --> 00:02:11,300
Hour one.

69
00:02:11,690 --> 00:02:13,040
Now money starts to notice.

70
00:02:13,670 --> 00:02:15,620
A tourist's card gets declined,

71
00:02:15,870 --> 00:02:17,740
because their bank is on another continent.

72
00:02:18,650 --> 00:02:19,950
Traders in New York can't

73
00:02:19,950 --> 00:02:21,200
see prices in London.

74
00:02:22,110 --> 00:02:23,390
Companies that keep their data

75
00:02:23,390 --> 00:02:25,380
in a cloud overseas go dark.

76
00:02:26,190 --> 00:02:28,080
Satellites try to carry the load.

77
00:02:28,290 --> 00:02:29,310
But that's a river trying

78
00:02:29,310 --> 00:02:30,740
to squeeze through a straw.

79
00:02:32,220 --> 00:02:33,210
And for some places,

80
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:33,950
it's worse.

81
00:02:34,810 --> 00:02:36,710
Islands with one or two cables are

82
00:02:36,710 --> 00:02:38,420
simply cut off from the world.

83
00:02:40,050 --> 00:02:40,700
Day one.

84
00:02:41,030 --> 00:02:42,260
Sounds like science fiction?

85
00:02:42,650 --> 00:02:43,880
Parts of it already happened.

86
00:02:44,430 --> 00:02:45,550
In 2008,

87
00:02:45,550 --> 00:02:47,830
cable cuts near Egypt knocked out about 70

88
00:02:47,830 --> 00:02:50,180
percent of Egypt's link to the wider internet.

89
00:02:50,790 --> 00:02:52,250
India lost roughly half

90
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:53,580
of its outbound connection.

91
00:02:54,610 --> 00:02:55,740
In 2024,

92
00:02:56,070 --> 00:02:57,290
a sinking cargo ship

93
00:02:57,290 --> 00:02:59,850
in the Red Sea most likely dragged its anchor

94
00:02:59,850 --> 00:03:01,840
and cut three cables at once.

95
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:03,660
And in 2022,

96
00:03:03,810 --> 00:03:05,710
an undersea volcano in Tonga snapped

97
00:03:05,710 --> 00:03:07,780
the country's only international cable.

98
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:09,920
The whole country fell back

99
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:11,770
on a handful of satellite links.

100
00:03:13,090 --> 00:03:13,900
Week one.

101
00:03:14,070 --> 00:03:15,400
So who fixes all this?

102
00:03:15,980 --> 00:03:17,540
Only about 20 to 30 ships

103
00:03:17,540 --> 00:03:20,110
on Earth are dedicated cable repair ships.

104
00:03:20,720 --> 00:03:22,120
A ship sails to the break

105
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:24,855
and drags a hook along the sea floor.

106
00:03:25,410 --> 00:03:27,480
It lifts the cable up to the deck,

107
00:03:27,590 --> 00:03:28,530
and the crew splices

108
00:03:28,530 --> 00:03:30,480
the glass back together by hand.

109
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:32,840
Even for a single break,

110
00:03:33,110 --> 00:03:34,510
the typical repair takes about

111
00:03:34,510 --> 00:03:36,120
eight days just to start.

112
00:03:37,290 --> 00:03:38,240
Now do the math.

113
00:03:38,550 --> 00:03:40,380
About 570 cables,

114
00:03:40,690 --> 00:03:42,000
about 25 ships.

115
00:03:42,610 --> 00:03:44,920
That's around 23 cables for every ship.

116
00:03:45,530 --> 00:03:47,240
At even two weeks each,

117
00:03:47,490 --> 00:03:48,650
the last ones come back

118
00:03:48,650 --> 00:03:50,060
almost a year later.

119
00:03:51,150 --> 00:03:52,040
Month one.

120
00:03:52,170 --> 00:03:54,000
Tonga's repair took five weeks.

121
00:03:54,850 --> 00:03:55,910
The crew had to replace

122
00:03:55,910 --> 00:03:57,980
about 56 miles of cable.

123
00:03:58,630 --> 00:03:59,450
Now picture that,

124
00:03:59,450 --> 00:04:00,470
at every cable,

125
00:04:00,470 --> 00:04:01,480
all at once.

126
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:04,420
So next time a message lands

127
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:05,800
on the other side of the world,

128
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:06,750
in a blink,

129
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:09,090
remember the trip it took:

130
00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:10,830
down to the ocean floor,

131
00:04:10,860 --> 00:04:13,310
through a glass thread inside a garden hose.

132
00:04:14,110 --> 00:04:15,350
And somewhere out there,

133
00:04:15,350 --> 00:04:17,200
a ship is fixing one right now.

134
00:04:17,950 --> 00:04:18,840
This is Tech,

135
00:04:19,050 --> 00:04:19,800
explained.

136
00:04:19,910 --> 00:04:20,320
Subscribe,

137
00:04:20,610 --> 00:04:22,160
and I'll show you how the rest of it works.

